WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.330
It's my pleasure to welcome to the program
today.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:05.730
Ask for Taylor, who is an author and documentary
filmmaker.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.460
She's the director of the film.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.460
What is democracy?

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.990
And also author of the book democracy may
not exist, but we'll miss it when it's gone.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.210
Uh, great to talk to you.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.240
I appreciate it.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.820
Hey, thanks for having me.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:24.779
So we've had conversations before, um, what
democracy, uh, is, should be and could be.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:29.900
And one of the conversations that often ends
up happening between, uh, the audience, uh,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:36.250
and me after those interviews are these questions
about, um, is direct democracy re, uh, really

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.890
the only type of democracy that we should
be going for and questions like that.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:45.101
And I find that a little bit less interesting
than something you're dealing with, which

00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:48.500
is the idea of is it really democracy?

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:55.680
If there is dark money, if there is disproportionate
power by Kleptocrats and corporations and

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.840
peoples in p people in positions of power,
et cetera.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.600
So to kind of start the conversation, has
there ever been a democracy?

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.200
And that could be a, uh, however we choose
to define it, that was not in some way influenced

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.970
by those forces, uh, by which many modern
Western democracies are influenced today.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.370
I think it's an interesting question.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.220
I mean, I've certainly been involved in those
debates you just laid out.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.220
Right?

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.299
You know, is, is the only real democracy direct
and, and not, you know, not using structures

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:32.350
of representation and, and so, um, that influences
a bit of, of the work I'm doing.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:36.750
But I tried to, uh, ask a different kind of
question.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:43.560
Actually I wanted to shift perspective and
to s to look at democracy as a process, as

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.829
an ongoing project.

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:47.950
And it kind of ideal that we're reaching towards.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:54.760
And one of the consistent things that has
thwarted, uh, people in their democratic aspirations

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.810
has been the problem of economic inequality.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.299
And this goes all the way back to ancient
Greece.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:05.870
So, you know, the ancient Athenians didn't
invent the practice of democracy, but they

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:09.800
gave us the word, uh, which comes down to
Deimos and Kratos.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.730
The people hold power or rule.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.290
And even in those early days, which, you know,
they didn't have a system that you could describe

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.400
as capitalism, right?

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.340
They didn't have a modern industrial economy
like, like we do.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:25.480
But nonetheless, they were very attuned to
the fact that any qualities of wealth would

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:26.510
subvert democracy.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:32.980
And in fact, in the republic, which is, you
know, the famous anti-democratic text by Plato,

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.380
um, where he famously warns that democracy
devolves into tyranny.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.530
He says very clearly that the underlying problem
is that the divide between the rich and the

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.050
poor grows to the point where it breaks the
city into where the rich want to get richer.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.660
And so they put poor people into debt and
the poor can't live anymore.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:54.281
Um, and so that this creates conflict and
leads to the rise of demagogues and what we

00:02:54.281 --> 00:02:57.510
would consider populism in today's terms.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:03.720
So I think with the problem of economic inequality
as something democracies have to wrestle with,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:09.410
and it creates all sorts of problems for the
way our political system is structured today.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:14.050
It, in my book, in, in, in the framing I use,
what I say is that there are these inherent

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.180
tensions in democracy there.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.470
Why democracy is, is because it involves contradictory
elements, right?

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:23.110
So the local and the global and the question
of who's in and who's out.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.710
And, uh, the question, how to balance the
needs of the present with the future, but

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:31.980
intense inequality, economic inequality just
makes those tensions almost, you know, unbearable

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.830
and really, um, uh, makes exacerbates, uh,
problems that we have to negotiate no matter

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:39.830
what.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.620
Yeah, I, um, when I, I'm reminded of when
I read Jared diamond's book, guns, germs and

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:48.780
steel, and in it he talks about, I think he
divides, um, uh, people as, as potentially

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:54.060
organizing in four different ways going from
I think it's individual villages, like groups

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.070
of families, relatively small.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:04:01.310
Um, and maybe the second one is tribes then
fiefdoms and then more sort of like nation

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.010
states or something closer to what we have
today.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:10.200
Uh, and once you get to the fiefdoms and the
nation states, it's really difficult to imagine

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.019
circumstances in which there is not going
to be some inequality, either be it economic

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:22.160
or in terms of cultural power, which often
comes from disproportionate economic power.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.400
Now there will be those who will hear us talking
and they'll say, no, you guys are missing

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.680
the reality that this is really an economic
conversation about socialism versus capitalism.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.480
And that you can have a democracy that is
far more equal, if not totally equal, if you

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.530
simply change your system of government to
one of socialism.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.730
But I actually think it's more complicated
than that.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.410
I'm curious to hear what you think about it.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:48.570
Well, you know, in the preface to the book
and throughout, I, I, you know, invoke, um,

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.590
the socialist project, right?

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.950
Because I think Socialists have played an
incredibly powerful role in, in, in advancing

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.410
democracy as we know it today.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.040
I mean, they were, they pushed for, for suffrage,
for civil rights, all sorts of things.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:07.480
Um, I, my, my point is that even in a system
of economic egalitarianism, right?

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:13.040
So even if we were able to close the gap between
the rich and the poor between owners and workers,

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.040
right?

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.010
And, and I personally have, I have an imagination
that allows me to imagine that.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.710
I mean, just like a, a hundred years ago,
someone might say, hey, masters and slaves.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:22.720
That's just natural.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:23.900
That's how it always is.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.270
You know, now we say, hey, there's makers
and takers.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.310
There's rich and poor.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:28.310
That's how it always is.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:32.750
I personally can imagine a world where we've
closed that gap because we know that we know

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:38.120
some countries have far less, uh, wild decrees
of any quality than the United States today.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:45.730
So, you know, I think we could do a lot to,
um, to lift people up and bring other people

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.250
down to a sane level.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.260
We don't need a world where we don't need
a country where three billionaires have as

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.390
much wealth as half of the, uh, population.

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:58.700
Uh, and so the thing I'm trying to say is
that the democracy part is actually the challenging

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:04.400
bit because even in a world we might call
socialist or in an economy that that is not

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.620
capitalism as we know it, we'd still have
to balance these factors.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:11.820
We'd still have to figure out, well, when
is cordial legitimate or when can people choose,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:12.820
right?

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:18.440
Uh, when do we, um, marshaled the, uh, expertise
of that only a few people have?

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:23.340
And when do we, um, uh, you know, harness
public opinion, right?

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.639
When, how do we balance the needs of people
who live today with people who have not yet

00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:27.639
been born?

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:31.400
So the book and that I think when we look
at things that way, we can say, okay, well

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.250
democracy is something that you know, is a
constant negotiation.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:35.700
We have to constantly reinvent it.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.370
We have to constantly think of new, well,
who is the people and how do they role?

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:44.510
Um, and so I don't think that, you know, socialism
is this magic word that would solve all of

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:45.510
our problems.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:50.451
My attitude is that actually if we had a more
economically fair system, if we had a system

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:54.900
where we finally had, you know, politically
quality, we were where we understood women

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.350
are equals of men and black people are equal
to white people.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.550
And you know, people without papers are equal
to people with papers.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:07.190
If we finally had that kind of, um, equality
that democracy actually requires and the economic

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.610
equality to support that political equality,
then we'd actually get to have really, really

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.610
interesting democratic debates.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.320
Because then we'd have to think about things
like, well, how do we have control?

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:21.630
Do we have communities that have, say over
the decisions that affect them, that have

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:25.480
influence over the decisions that affect their
lives, while also taking into account the

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:26.750
broader world, right?

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:31.919
How do we balance the needs of, uh, the present
and those who are not yet born, right?

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:33.060
That's the problem of climate change.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.950
So I think that, um, democracy is always going
to be, it's always going to be difficult.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.370
Um, and, but hopefully those challenges will
just get more interesting.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.370
Yeah.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.840
In thinking about the broader world, I mean,
I hesitate to talk about the, anytime you

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.160
introduce the term, what is natural?

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.550
I mean, remember the, around the gay marriage
debate, how problematic this concept of what,

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:58.260
what is natural a was, but to think at least
hypothetically for a moment, is the idea of

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:03.760
people being democratic, unnatural or an unnatural
idea.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.280
When we think back to humans, like in a state
of nature, hypothetically.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.070
So when you think about how humans interact
at some of those smaller scales in bands of

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:16.250
a few families is what might work or not work.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:21.730
They're not necessarily relatable or translatable
to populations of millions or even hundreds

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:24.530
of millions of people as we now have in some
of the larger countries.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:27.680
That's a loaded question given the history
of political philosophy, right?

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:33.360
Which is always invoked the state of nature,
this hypothetical, a situation where, you

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.900
know, people were either a warring and defensive
in a hobsy incense or actually living in peace

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:42.660
without, um, private property in a [inaudible]
sense.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:47.040
Say, I'm curious about this, but I'm wondering
if it's even the right question to be.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.710
Yeah, I live in question.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:52.060
I think it's, you know, I think, well, it's
interesting to me the way, you know, political

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:57.940
philosophy, um, promotes different ideologies
and, and it comes in moments of political

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.590
rupture and it helps people think through
the problems of their day.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:01.770
I would frame things differently.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:06.220
I mean, I think if you look at human history,
there's all this contradictory evidence, right?

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:11.470
The history of democracy is, you know, a history
of colonial conquest imperialism.

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.950
I mean, it's, it's, we in a nation that calls
itself the land of freedom that was founded

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:18.810
on the ultimate form of unfreedom slavery.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:25.480
Um, you know, people have made really terrible
choices in the, in, uh, in the past, but,

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.570
you know, then you can also look back at human
history, democratic history and see that there

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:31.730
are all sorts of wonderful advances, right?

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.490
That people have, um, had solidarity, they've
set up for each other.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:40.400
That actually this circle of inclusion is,
you know, expanding and if it's, it's, um,

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:44.510
imperfect and, and, um, and sometimes progress
goes into reverse.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:49.400
So I, I think the jury is still out and that
we shouldn't extrapolate too much from sort

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.170
of, you know, some idea of a state of nature.

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:56.130
I, I liked it from the question actually,
to go back to Rousseau in terms of what was

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:59.950
called the founding paradox and as this founding
paradox of political philosophy, and it's

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:03.770
how do you make a democratic people out of
an undemocratic people.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:09.170
And you know, if we don't naturally inclined
towards, you know, democracy especially in,

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:14.890
uh, a modern industrial, uh, you know, global
system, like the one we live in, we have to

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:19.400
do is we have to create the conditions for
us to be democratic through culture, through

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.240
education, through organizations of solidarity.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.170
We have to nurture a democratic ethos.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:29.871
And that's our challenge I think, as, as part
of the many, as part of regular, you know,

00:10:29.871 --> 00:10:31.670
as part of a community of regular people.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:35.250
I think we also live in a system where the
powerful have exactly the opposite agenda.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:39.690
What they want to do is create an undemocratic
people out of a democratically spirited people.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:45.050
So that's why, you know, famously in the late
sixties, you know, Samuel Huntington and others

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:50.120
said there was a crisis of democracy when,
when youth were revolting and, and, um, and

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.610
there was too much sort of labor unrest, what
they were like, oh no, there's, you know,

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.180
for, for elites, when there's too much democracy,
it's a crisis.

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:59.040
They want people to become more undemocratic,
more complacent.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.980
So, you know, I think we are, we're certainly
standing in a moment where there's a kind

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:10.640
of, um, democratic inclusion that, that people,
um, not that long ago would have thought was,

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:11.640
you know, impossible.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.640
Right?

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.700
I mean, people said, you know, women were
totally incapable of, of rational in judgment

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:20.220
and participation and needed to have, you
know, their husband speak for them.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:21.760
So we don't know what's possible.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.790
Um, and I think invoking human nature is,
yeah, often, uh, not the best way to go.

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:31.899
How can we interpret in thinking about this
issue, what we now know to be a reality that

00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:36.920
often democracies can make the wrong choices
or at least uninformed choices or sometimes

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.850
ignorant choices or whatever the case may
be.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.339
And whether we apply it to the current administration
or whatever else, it doesn't really matter.

00:11:43.339 --> 00:11:48.270
But conceptually, I mean, even going back,
uh, to, to some of the older teachings that

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:56.180
you cite in your work, um, there is this problem
where oftentimes the, the populace, uh, of

00:11:56.180 --> 00:12:03.320
the more populous countries will have uprisings
or, um, social social moves or changes that

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:06.930
lead to bad decisions being made within the
system.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:08.390
How should we think about that more broadly?

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.160
At a political philosophy level?

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.910
Yeah, I mean, I think it's a risk that we
have to take.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:15.320
I would never argue that democracy isn't risky.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:19.980
The Deimos can make bad choices, but I think
just, you know, again, just as we might say,

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.149
okay, there's all this evidence that we can't
trust the Deimos that they are, this unruly

00:12:24.149 --> 00:12:28.720
mob played a warned of, you know, that the
democracy marginalizes the wise and the virtuous,

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:29.950
which is what he said in the republic.

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:35.440
And we also have to say the best ideas about
democracy came from people came from people

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:36.490
who were excluded, right?

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:39.100
When women said we should be included as equal
citizens.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:44.050
They were outside the community, outside the
political community, ideas about what democracy

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:47.850
could be, have always come from the margins
from oppressant and outsiders.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:52.450
And the other thing is that, you know, we've,
we've tried technocracy that's sort of been

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:57.480
what we've, the system, you know, that's some
people talk about, uh, neoliberal capitalism

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.310
is one where the technocrats rule, right?

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:02.550
They'll sort of put the experts in charge
and, and they'll make decisions.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:08.210
And, you know, we've, what we've had is a,
uh, an intense, you know, escalation in terms

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.570
of the concentration of wealth.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:14.459
We've had, um, you know, a systems emerge
that are too big to fail except the fact they

00:13:14.459 --> 00:13:15.459
can fail.

00:13:15.459 --> 00:13:20.310
What we're facing is a climate crisis that
even though the science says this is going

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:25.390
to be devastated and in fact, it already is
devastating for, for 'em communities, climate

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.690
change is happening now.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.450
You know, we, we seem unable to stop it.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:34.150
So, you know, putting experts in charge, um,
you know, uh, and demand, you know, diminishing

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:35.680
the power of the people hasn't worked either.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:41.410
So that's where I think, um, we have to, again,
create structures where people can, can become

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:42.410
more informed.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:45.410
This is why education is so central to, to
the democratic project.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:49.649
Um, we also have to create a media ecosystem
that has positive incentives.

00:13:49.649 --> 00:13:53.610
Before this book, I wrote a book called the
People's platform about the political economy

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.930
of the Internet and how, you know, so much.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:14:02.440
The problem with it is that the incentives
are all towards clickbait and fast turning

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:09.209
content because, you know, it's the, the business
model is advertising is, is, um, is data collection,

00:14:09.209 --> 00:14:10.209
right?

00:14:10.209 --> 00:14:15.740
So, you know, we're, we are, we're, we have
to address these sort of underlying economic

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:21.080
arrangements that shape our culture and in,
and, you know, distort our democracy.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:24.100
